I make the helper generic over a zod schema so the static type is inferred from the same object that performs runtime validation.

import { z } from 'zod';
export class ApiError extends Error {
  constructor(public status: number, public body: unknown) { super(`HTTP ${status}`); }
}
export async function apiFetch<S extends z.ZodTypeAny>(url: string, schema: S, init?: RequestInit): Promise<z.infer<S>> {
  const res = await fetch(url, { ...init, headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', ...init?.headers } });
  const body: unknown = res.status === 204 ? null : await res.json().catch(() => null);
  if (!res.ok) throw new ApiError(res.status, body);
  return schema.parse(body);
}

Usage: const user = await apiFetch('/api/users/1', UserSchema, { signal }); and user is typed as z.infer<typeof UserSchema>. Passing init through lets callers supply an AbortSignal. The work is linear in the size of the response body for both parsing and validation, so time and space are O(n). Pitfalls: fetch only rejects on network failures, not on 4xx or 5xx, which is why the res.ok check matters, and calling res.json() on a 204 throws, so I guard it.

The hook stores a delayed copy of the value and resets a timer whenever the input changes, cleaning up the pending timer on each change and on unmount.

import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
export function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs = 300): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);
  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delayMs]);
  return debounced;
}

In the search component: const [term, setTerm] = useState(''); const q = useDebouncedValue(term, 300); const { data } = useQuery({ queryKey: ['search', q], queryFn: ({ signal }) => searchApi(q, signal), enabled: q.length > 1 }). The input stays responsive because term updates immediately, while the network only sees q. Each keystroke does O(1) work, scheduling and clearing one timer, and space is O(1). The generic T means it works for strings, numbers or filter objects, but objects must be stable references or the timer resets every render. Passing the AbortSignal also cancels in-flight requests when q changes.

I render the page inside a fresh QueryClient, override the MSW handler to return an error, and assert on what the user sees.

import { render, screen } from '@testing-library/react';
import { QueryClient, QueryClientProvider } from '@tanstack/react-query';
import { http, HttpResponse } from 'msw';
import { server } from '../test/server';
import { OrdersPage } from './OrdersPage';

function renderWithClient(ui: React.ReactElement) {
  const client = new QueryClient({ defaultOptions: { queries: { retry: false } } });
  return render(<QueryClientProvider client={client}>{ui}</QueryClientProvider>);
}

test('shows API error detail', async () => {
  server.use(http.get('/api/orders', () => HttpResponse.json({ detail: 'Orders service unavailable' }, { status: 503 })));
  renderWithClient(<OrdersPage />);
  expect(screen.getByRole('status')).toHaveTextContent(/loading/i);
  expect(await screen.findByRole('alert')).toHaveTextContent('Orders service unavailable');
});

Key choices: retry false stops TanStack Query retrying three times and slowing the test; a new client per test prevents cache leakage between tests; and role-based queries assume the component uses role status and alert, which also improves accessibility. Runtime is dominated by rendering, effectively O(1) for this fixed tree. Pitfall: asserting the loading state with findBy instead of getBy can miss it because it resolves after the response.
